280	35	Relative Clauses - Who, Which, That, Where, When	Defining vs Non-defining relative clauses	DEFINING (especificativa) - sin comas:\n- Identifica de qué/quién hablamos\n- Información esencial\n- THAT puede usarse\n- El pronombre puede omitirse si es objeto\n\n"The woman who lives next door is nice."\n(¿Cuál mujer? La que vive al lado)\n\nNON-DEFINING (explicativa) - CON comas:\n- Añade información extra\n- Información no esencial\n- THAT no puede usarse\n- El pronombre NUNCA se omite\n\n"My mother, who is 60, still works."\n(Ya sabemos quién es, añadimos info)	The car that I bought is red. (defining) My car, which is red, is fast. (non-defining)	\N	"Paris, which is in France, is beautiful. The book I read was good."	"My mother that is 60... \\u274c Paris that is in France... \\u274c"	"Usar THAT en non-defining u olvidar las comas."	Los pronombres relativos como objeto pueden omitirse en defining clauses.	3	2026-02-04 04:56:29.71358
281	36	Passive Voice - Present and Past	Form: BE + past participle	Formación de la voz pasiva:\n\nActiva → Pasiva:\nSubject + Verb + Object → Object + BE + Past Participle (+ by + Agent)\n\nPRESENTE SIMPLE:\n- Active: They make cars in Japan.\n- Passive: Cars are made in Japan.\n\nPASADO SIMPLE:\n- Active: Someone stole my bike.\n- Passive: My bike was stolen.\n\nPRESENTE PERFECTO:\n- Active: They have finished the project.\n- Passive: The project has been finished.\n\nFUTURO:\n- Active: They will announce the winner.\n- Passive: The winner will be announced.	English is spoken here. The window was broken by the ball.	\N	"The letter was written yesterday. These phones are made in China."	"The book was wrote. \\u274c Spanish is speak here. \\u274c"	"Usar el infinitivo o pasado simple en lugar del participio."	GET + past participle es más informal: He got fired.	1	2026-02-04 04:56:29.714623
