290	52	Wish and If Only	Wish/If only + past simple for present regrets	Deseos sobre el presente (situación irreal ahora):\n\nWISH/IF ONLY + Past Simple\n- I wish I had more money. (no tengo suficiente)\n- If only I knew the answer. (no lo sé)\n- I wish I could help. (no puedo)\n\nCon BE, usamos WERE (formal) para todos:\n- I wish I were taller.\n- If only she were here.\n\nEsto expresa insatisfacción con el presente:\n- I wish my apartment were bigger.\n  = I'm not happy because my apartment is small.	I wish I spoke French. If only I had a car.	\N	"I wish I knew her name. If only he were more patient."	"I wish I have more time. \\u274c I wish I can swim. \\u274c"	"Usar presente en lugar de pasado con wish."	Was es aceptable en inglés informal: I wish I was there.	1	2026-02-04 04:56:29.731601
291	52	Wish and If Only	Wish/If only + past perfect for past regrets	Deseos/arrepentimientos sobre el pasado:\n\nWISH/IF ONLY + Past Perfect\n- I wish I had studied harder. (no estudié)\n- If only I hadn't said that. (lo dije)\n- I wish I had listened to you. (no escuché)\n\nEsto expresa arrepentimiento - quisieras haber hecho algo diferente:\n- I wish I had bought that house.\n  = I regret not buying that house.\n\n- If only I hadn't eaten so much.\n  = I regret eating so much.	I wish I had accepted the job offer. If only I'd known earlier.	\N	"She wishes she had traveled more when young."	"I wish I studied harder. \\u274c (para el pasado)"	"Usar past simple para arrepentimientos pasados."	If only es más enfático/emocional que wish.	2	2026-02-04 04:56:29.733211
292	52	Wish and If Only	Wish + would for complaints and requests	Deseos sobre acciones de otros (queja/petición):\n\nWISH + Subject + WOULD + base verb\n- I wish you would listen to me. (no me escuchas)\n- I wish it would stop raining. (sigue lloviendo)\n- If only he would call. (no llama)\n\nUso:\n1. Quejas sobre comportamiento repetido:\n   - I wish she wouldn't smoke inside.\n   \n2. Deseo de que algo cambie:\n   - I wish they would fix the road.\n\nNUNCA: I wish I would...\n(usamos wish + past simple para nosotros mismos)	I wish you would be quiet. If only the neighbors would turn down the music.	\N	"I wish he would stop complaining."	"I wish I would be taller. \\u274c"	"Usar wish + would con uno mismo."	Para eventos naturales/incontrolables: I wish it would rain.	3	2026-02-04 04:56:29.734901
293	53	Inversion for Emphasis	Negative adverbs at the beginning: Never have I...	Inversión con adverbios negativos:\n\nCuando ponemos adverbios negativos al inicio, invertimos sujeto y auxiliar:\n\nNEVER + aux + subject + verb\n- Never have I seen such a thing.\n- Never had she felt so happy.\n\nRARELY/SELDOM + aux + subject + verb\n- Rarely do we see such talent.\n- Seldom have I met someone so kind.\n\nHARDLY/BARELY/SCARCELY + aux + subject + verb\n- Hardly had I arrived when the phone rang.\n- Scarcely had she finished when...\n\nNO SOONER...THAN\n- No sooner had I left than it started raining.	Never have I been so embarrassed. Rarely does he make mistakes.	\N	"Seldom do they visit. Hardly had we begun when..."	"Never I have seen... \\u274c Rarely he does... \\u274c"	"Mantener el orden normal despu\\u00e9s de adverbio negativo."	Esto es formal/literario; en conversación normal se evita.	1	2026-02-04 04:56:29.737135
294	53	Inversion for Emphasis	Only + time/after/when: Only then did I realize...	Inversión con ONLY + expresiones:\n\nONLY + time expression + aux + subject\n- Only yesterday did I hear the news.\n- Only later did she understand.\n\nONLY + after/when + normal clause, + inverted clause\n- Only after the meeting did we discuss it.\n- Only when I saw him did I believe it.\n\nONLY + by + -ing + aux + subject\n- Only by working hard can you succeed.\n\nNOT ONLY...BUT ALSO (inversión en la primera parte)\n- Not only did he win, but he also broke the record.	Only then did I understand. Not only is she smart, but also kind.	\N	"Only after reading it did I see the error."	"Only then I understood. \\u274c"	"Olvidar invertir despu\\u00e9s de \\"only + expression\\"."	Sin expresión, "only" no causa inversión: I only saw her once.	2	2026-02-04 04:56:29.739208
295	54	Cleft Sentences for Emphasis	IT + BE + focus + that/who clause	Oraciones hendidas con IT:\n\nEstructura: It + BE + elemento enfatizado + that/who + resto\n\nOriginal: John broke the window.\n\nEnfatizando diferentes elementos:\n- It was JOHN who broke the window. (quién)\n- It was THE WINDOW that John broke. (qué)\n- It was YESTERDAY that John broke the window. (cuándo)\n\nUsos:\n- Contrastar información\n- Corregir malentendidos\n- Dar información sorprendente\n\n"It wasn't me who ate the cake, it was Tom!"	It was in Paris that they first met. It's the noise that bothers me.	\N	"It was Mary who told me. It's this song that I love."	"It was John what broke it. \\u274c"	"Usar what en lugar de that/who."	Con personas, who es preferible pero that es aceptable.	1	2026-02-04 04:56:29.741692
296	54	Cleft Sentences for Emphasis	What clause + BE + focus	Oraciones hendidas con WHAT (pseudo-cleft):\n\nEstructura: What + clause + BE + elemento enfatizado\n\nOriginal: I need a vacation.\nÉnfasis: What I need is a vacation.\n\nOriginal: She said something strange.\nÉnfasis: What she said was strange.\n\nVariaciones:\n- What I want is peace.\n- What happened was terrible.\n- What you should do is apologize.\n\nTHE THING THAT... también funciona:\n- The thing that annoys me is his attitude.\n- The reason why I left was the noise.	What I like most is the view. What she needs is rest.	\N	"What bothers me is the price. What he said was rude."	"What I need are money. \\u274c"	"Problemas de concordancia con el verbo BE."	Con plurales: What I bought were books. (formal)	2	2026-02-04 04:56:29.744459
297	55	Advanced Modal Verbs	Modals of deduction: must/can't/might + have + past participle	Deducciones sobre el pasado:\n\nMUST HAVE + PP = casi seguro que pasó\n- He must have forgotten. (Estoy casi seguro)\n- They must have left early.\n\nCAN'T/COULDN'T HAVE + PP = imposible que pasó\n- She can't have said that. (Es imposible)\n- He couldn't have known.\n\nMAY/MIGHT/COULD HAVE + PP = posiblemente pasó\n- She might have missed the train.\n- They could have gotten lost.\n\nSHOULD HAVE / OUGHT TO HAVE + PP = era esperado pero no pasó\n- He should have called by now.\n- They ought to have arrived.	She must have been tired. He can't have known the answer.	\N	"They might have forgotten. You shouldn't have worried."	"He must forgot. \\u274c She can't said that. \\u274c"	"Olvidar \\"have\\" o usar el infinitivo en lugar del participio."	Needn't have = no era necesario pero se hizo: You needn't have bought flowers.	1	2026-02-04 04:56:29.749056
298	55	Advanced Modal Verbs	Would for past habits vs Used to	WOULD vs USED TO para hábitos pasados:\n\nUSED TO: hábitos Y estados pasados\n- I used to live in Paris. (estado)\n- I used to play tennis. (hábito)\n\nWOULD: solo hábitos pasados (no estados)\n- Every summer, we would go to the beach.\n- She would always bring me coffee.\n\nNO se puede usar WOULD con:\n- Estados: I would live in Paris ❌\n- Verbos de estado: She would know him ❌\n\nWOULD suena más nostálgico/narrativo:\n"When I was young, I would spend hours reading..."	We would visit grandma every Sunday. I used to be afraid of dogs.	\N	"She would always make us laugh. I used to have a cat."	"I would live in London (as a state). \\u274c"	"Usar would con verbos de estado o para situaciones no habituales."	Would también expresa voluntad pasada: He wouldn't listen to me.	2	2026-02-04 04:56:29.752395
299	56	Subjunctive Mood	Formal subjunctive after verbs of suggestion/demand	El subjuntivo formal (base form) después de ciertos verbos:\n\nVerbos que requieren subjuntivo:\nsuggest, recommend, demand, insist, request, require, propose, ask\n\nEstructura: Verb + (that) + subject + BASE FORM\n- I suggest (that) he leave now.\n- They demanded (that) she be present.\n- It is essential (that) everyone attend.\n\nNOTA: usamos la forma base para todos los sujetos:\n- I suggest he GO (no "goes")\n- She insisted I BE there (no "am/was")\n\nEn inglés británico, "should" es alternativa:\n- I suggest he should leave.	The doctor recommended that she take more rest. It's vital that he be informed.	\N	"I insisted that he pay me back. It's important that this be done."	"I suggest that he leaves. \\u274c (en formal)"	"Usar formas conjugadas en lugar de la base."	El subjuntivo es más común en inglés americano formal.	1	2026-02-04 04:56:29.756084
300	56	Subjunctive Mood	Subjunctive in fixed expressions	Subjuntivo en expresiones fijas:\n\nExpresiones comunes con subjuntivo:\n- God bless you!\n- God forbid!\n- Long live the king!\n- Heaven help us!\n- Be that as it may...\n- Come what may...\n- Suffice it to say...\n- Be it known that...\n- Far be it from me to...\n- If need be...\n\nTambién en condicionales formales:\n- If I were you... (were, no was)\n- If it be necessary... (muy formal)\n- Were I to know... (inversión formal)	Be that as it may, we must continue. Far be it from me to criticize.	\N	"Long live the queen! Come what may, I'll be there."	"God blesses you! \\u274c"	"No reconocer estas expresiones como subjuntivo."	Muchas de estas expresiones son arcaicas pero aún se usan.	2	2026-02-04 04:56:29.760291
301	57	Participle Clauses	Present participle (-ing) for simultaneous or causal actions	Cláusulas de participio presente:\n\nAcciones simultáneas:\n- Walking down the street, I saw an old friend.\n  (= While I was walking...)\n\nCausa/razón:\n- Being tired, she went to bed early.\n  (= Because she was tired...)\n\nDespués de conjunciones:\n- After finishing dinner, we watched a movie.\n- Before leaving, check your pockets.\n- While waiting, I read a book.\n\nEl sujeto debe ser el mismo en ambas partes:\n✓ Seeing the rain, I took an umbrella. (I saw, I took)\n✗ Seeing the rain, the umbrella was taken. (misplaced modifier)	Having finished work, she went home. Not knowing what to do, he called for help.	\N	"Standing at the window, I noticed a strange car."	"Walking to school, the rain started. \\u274c"	"Participio colgante (dangling modifier) - sujetos diferentes."	Algunos participios colgantes son aceptables: Generally speaking, Considering...	1	2026-02-04 04:56:29.764204
302	57	Participle Clauses	Past participle for passive meaning	Cláusulas de participio pasado (sentido pasivo):\n\nEstructura: Past Participle + (by agent), main clause\n\nEjemplos:\n- Written in 1605, the play is still popular.\n  (= The play, which was written in 1605...)\n  \n- Exhausted by the journey, they fell asleep immediately.\n  (= Because they were exhausted...)\n\n- Shocked by the news, she couldn't speak.\n  (= She was shocked by the news, so...)\n\nPuede ir al principio o después del sujeto:\n- The painting, discovered in 1920, is priceless.\n- Discovered in 1920, the painting is priceless.	Built in 1920, the house is now a museum. Surprised by the question, he hesitated.	\N	"Seen from above, the city looks beautiful."	"Seeing from above, the city... \\u274c (la ciudad no ve)"	"Confundir cu\\u00e1ndo usar -ing (activo) vs -ed (pasivo)."	Algunos participios funcionan como adjetivos: concerned, interested.	2	2026-02-04 04:56:29.768067
303	57	Participle Clauses	Perfect participle for prior actions	Participio perfecto para acciones anteriores:\n\nHAVING + Past Participle = después de / porque antes\n\n- Having finished the exam, she left.\n  (= After she had finished...)\n  \n- Having lived in Japan, he speaks Japanese.\n  (= Because he has lived...)\n\nVersión negativa: NOT HAVING + PP\n- Not having heard the news, I was surprised.\n\nVersión pasiva: HAVING BEEN + PP\n- Having been warned, he was careful.\n- Having been rejected twice, she gave up.	Having eaten, we continued our journey. Having been invited, I felt I should go.	\N	"Not having slept well, I was tired all day."	"Having finished, the work was good. \\u274c"	"Usar having + base form o having + past tense."	Having said that = dicho esto (expresión fija)	3	2026-02-04 04:56:29.771824
304	12	There is / There are	THERE IS for singular, THERE ARE for plural	Uso de There is/are para expresar existencia:\n\nTHERE IS + singular/uncountable:\n- There is a book on the table.\n- There is some water in the glass.\n- There is a problem.\n\nTHERE ARE + plural:\n- There are two cats in the garden.\n- There are many people here.\n- There are some books on the shelf.\n\nContracciones:\n- There's = There is (muy común)\n- There're = There are (menos común, informal)\n\nNegativo: There isn't / There aren't\nPregunta: Is there...? / Are there...?	There is a bank near here. There are 30 students in the class.	\N	"There's a cat outside. There are many options."	"There are a book. \\u274c It has a bank near here. \\u274c"	"Usar \\"It is\\" o \\"Have\\" para expresar existencia."	Informal: "There's" se usa a veces con plurales: There's two reasons.	1	2026-02-04 05:00:47.078846
305	12	There is / There are	Questions and short answers with There is/are	Preguntas con There is/are:\n\nPreguntas Sí/No:\n- Is there a bathroom here?\n  Yes, there is. / No, there isn't.\n- Are there any restaurants nearby?\n  Yes, there are. / No, there aren't.\n\nPreguntas con HOW MANY (contables):\n- How many chairs are there?\n  There are six chairs.\n\nPreguntas con HOW MUCH (incontables):\n- How much milk is there?\n  There's a little milk.\n\nCon ANY (preguntas y negativos):\n- Is there any coffee?\n- There aren't any shops.\n\nCon SOME (afirmativo):\n- There are some cookies.	Is there a gym in this hotel? How many bedrooms are there?	\N	"Are there any questions? There isn't any sugar."	"Have there any coffee? \\u274c Is there any books? \\u274c"	"Confundir some/any o usar singular/plural incorrectamente."	Some en preguntas cuando ofreces: Would you like some tea?	2	2026-02-04 05:00:47.081558
306	13	Demonstratives - This, That, These, Those	THIS/THESE for near, THAT/THOSE for far	Demostrativos según distancia:\n\nCERCA del hablante:\n- THIS (singular): This is my book.\n- THESE (plural): These are my friends.\n\nLEJOS del hablante:\n- THAT (singular): That is your house.\n- THOSE (plural): Those are his shoes.\n\nTambién para tiempo:\n- THIS = ahora/presente: this week, this year\n- THAT = pasado/futuro: that day, that summer\n\nComo adjetivos (antes de sustantivo):\n- This car is fast.\n- Those people are nice.\n\nComo pronombres (solos):\n- This is delicious!\n- I don't like those.	This is my phone. That building is very tall. These cookies are good.	\N	"Is this your bag? Who are those people over there?"	"This books \\u274c (these books). That are my friends. \\u274c"	"No concordar singular/plural con el sustantivo."	En el teléfono: "This is John" (presentarse), "Who is this?"	1	2026-02-04 05:00:47.083248
307	14	Object Pronouns - Me, You, Him, Her, It, Us, Them	Object pronouns come after verbs and prepositions	Pronombres de objeto:\n\nSubject → Object:\nI → me\nyou → you\nhe → him\nshe → her\nit → it\nwe → us\nthey → them\n\nPosición: después del verbo o preposición\n- She loves me. (después del verbo)\n- This is for you. (después de preposición)\n- Tell him the truth.\n- Give her the book.\n- I saw them yesterday.\n\nNUNCA como sujeto:\n✓ She and I went. (sujeto)\n✗ Me and her went. (incorrecto)	Call me later. I gave it to her. They saw us at the park.	\N	"Listen to me. He told them a story. Wait for us."	"She loves I. \\u274c Give the book to she. \\u274c"	"Usar pronombres de sujeto como objeto."	Informal: "It's me" es común, aunque "It is I" es gramaticalmente correcto.	1	2026-02-04 05:00:47.084625
308	15	Basic Question Words - What, Where, When, Who, Why, How	WH-Questions structure and meaning	Palabras interrogativas:\n\nWHAT = qué (cosas, acciones)\n- What is your name?\n- What do you do?\n\nWHERE = dónde (lugar)\n- Where do you live?\n- Where is the station?\n\nWHEN = cuándo (tiempo)\n- When is your birthday?\n- When does the class start?\n\nWHO = quién (personas)\n- Who is that man?\n- Who called you?\n\nWHY = por qué (razón)\n- Why are you late?\n- Why did you leave?\n\nHOW = cómo (manera, estado)\n- How are you?\n- How do you spell it?\n\nEstructura: WH-word + auxiliary + subject + verb	What time is it? Where does she work? Who is your teacher?	\N	"When does the movie start? How do you get there?"	"What you want? \\u274c Where he lives? \\u274c"	"Olvidar el auxiliar en preguntas."	Who/What como sujeto no necesitan auxiliar: Who wants coffee?	1	2026-02-04 05:00:47.085841
309	15	Basic Question Words - What, Where, When, Who, Why, How	HOW + adjective/adverb for degree questions	HOW + adjetivo/adverbio:\n\nHOW + adjective:\n- How old are you? (edad)\n- How tall is he? (altura)\n- How big is your house? (tamaño)\n- How long is the movie? (duración/longitud)\n- How far is the airport? (distancia)\n\nHOW + adverb:\n- How often do you exercise? (frecuencia)\n- How well do you speak English? (calidad)\n- How fast can you run? (velocidad)\n\nOtras expresiones:\n- How much? (cantidad incontable/precio)\n- How many? (cantidad contable)\n- How come? = Why? (informal)	How old is your sister? How far is the beach? How often do you travel?	\N	"How much does it cost? How many languages do you speak?"	"How many water? \\u274c How much people? \\u274c"	"Confundir how much/how many."	How come no invierte: How come you're late? (no "How come are you late?")	2	2026-02-04 05:00:47.087158
