311	16	Can for Ability and Requests	CAN/COULD for polite requests	CAN/COULD para peticiones:\n\nCAN (menos formal):\n- Can I borrow your pen?\n- Can you open the window?\n- Can we sit here?\n\nCOULD (más formal/cortés):\n- Could I use your phone?\n- Could you help me, please?\n- Could we have the bill?\n\nRespuestas:\n- Yes, of course. / Sure. / No problem.\n- I'm sorry, I can't. / I'm afraid not.\n\nPara pedir permiso:\n- Can I go to the bathroom?\n- Could I leave early today?	Can I ask you a question? Could you repeat that, please?	\N	"Can I have some water? Could you speak more slowly?"	"Can I to go? \\u274c Could you to help me? \\u274c"	"A\\u00f1adir \\"to\\" despu\\u00e9s de can/could."	May I es aún más formal: May I come in?	2	2026-02-04 05:00:47.089954
312	17	Telling the Time	O'clock, half past, quarter past/to	Formas de decir la hora:\n\nHora exacta: O'CLOCK\n- 3:00 = three o'clock\n- 9:00 = nine o'clock\n\nMedia hora: HALF PAST\n- 3:30 = half past three\n- 9:30 = half past nine\n\nCuartos: QUARTER PAST / QUARTER TO\n- 3:15 = quarter past three\n- 3:45 = quarter to four\n\nMinutos: PAST (hasta :30) / TO (después de :30)\n- 3:10 = ten past three\n- 3:50 = ten to four\n- 3:25 = twenty-five past three\n\nAM/PM o uso de 24h:\n- 3:00 PM = three o'clock in the afternoon\n- 15:00 = fifteen hundred (formal)	It's half past two. The meeting is at quarter to nine.	\N	"It's ten past eight. We leave at quarter past seven."	"It's three and half. \\u274c It's fifteen to. \\u274c"	"Olvidar especificar la hora con \\"to\\"."	Digital style: It's three thirty (3:30), four forty-five (4:45)	1	2026-02-04 05:00:47.091294
