314	7	Likes and Dislikes	Like/Love/Hate + noun or -ing form	Expresar gustos:\n\nVERBOS + sustantivo:\n- I like pizza.\n- She loves music.\n- They hate traffic.\n\nVERBOS + verb-ing:\n- I like reading.\n- She loves dancing.\n- He hates waiting.\n\nEscala de preferencia:\nlove > really like > like > don't mind > don't like > hate\n\nPreguntas:\n- Do you like coffee?\n- Does she like swimming?\n\nRespuestas:\n- Yes, I love it!\n- No, I don't like it at all.\n- It's okay. / I don't mind it.	I love cooking. She doesn't like getting up early.	\N	"Do you like watching movies? I really like Italian food."	"I like read books. \\u274c She like dancing. \\u274c"	"Usar infinitivo sin \\"to\\" o olvidar -s en tercera persona."	Like + to infinitive también existe: I like to read before bed.	1	2026-02-04 05:00:47.093834
315	20	Present Continuous for Now and Future	Form: am/is/are + verb-ing for actions happening now	Present Continuous para acciones en progreso:\n\nEstructura: am/is/are + verb-ing\n\nAfirmativo:\n- I am working.\n- She is sleeping.\n- They are playing.\n\nNegativo: am not / isn't / aren't + verb-ing\n- I'm not watching TV.\n- He isn't listening.\n\nPregunta: Am/Is/Are + subject + verb-ing?\n- Are you coming?\n- Is she studying?\n\nOrtografía del -ing:\n- Mayoría: + ing (playing, eating)\n- Terminados en -e: quitar e + ing (making, having)\n- CVC cortos: doblar + ing (running, swimming)\n- -ie: ie → y + ing (dying, lying)	I'm writing an email right now. Look! It's raining.	\N	"She's having breakfast. They aren't working today."	"She working now. \\u274c He is make dinner. \\u274c"	"Olvidar be o no a\\u00f1adir -ing."	Verbos de estado no usan continuo: I'm knowing ❌	1	2026-02-04 05:00:47.09586
316	20	Present Continuous for Now and Future	Present Continuous for future arrangements	Present Continuous para planes futuros:\n\nPara planes CONFIRMADOS con hora/lugar:\n- I'm meeting John tomorrow at 3.\n- We're flying to Paris next week.\n- She's starting a new job on Monday.\n\nDiferencia con GOING TO:\n- Going to = intención general\n- Present Continuous = plan confirmado con detalles\n\nEjemplos:\n- I'm going to travel more this year. (intención)\n- I'm traveling to Japan next month. (boleto comprado)\n\nVerbos comunes para futuro:\ncome, go, leave, arrive, meet, have, start, fly, visit	I'm having dinner with my parents tonight. We're leaving at 8 AM.	\N	"Are you working tomorrow? She's arriving on Friday."	"I'm travel next week. \\u274c (sin arreglo confirmado)"	"Usar para intenciones sin plan confirmado."	Siempre necesita expresión de tiempo futuro para claridad.	2	2026-02-04 05:00:47.097503
317	21	Some, Any, No, Every Compounds	SOME- for affirmative, ANY- for negative/questions	Compuestos con some/any/no/every:\n\nSOME- (afirmativo):\n- someone/somebody = alguien\n- something = algo\n- somewhere = en algún lugar\n- I saw someone at the door.\n\nANY- (negativo/preguntas):\n- anyone/anybody = alguien/nadie\n- anything = algo/nada\n- anywhere = en algún lugar/ningún lugar\n- Did you see anyone? I didn't hear anything.\n\nNO- (negativo fuerte):\n- no one/nobody = nadie\n- nothing = nada\n- nowhere = en ningún lugar\n- Nobody knows the answer.\n\nEVERY- (todos):\n- everyone/everybody = todos\n- everything = todo\n- everywhere = en todas partes\n- Everyone is here.	There's something in my bag. Is anyone home? Nobody answered.	\N	"I didn't see anybody. Everything is ready."	"I saw anyone. \\u274c Nobody didn't come. \\u274c"	"Usar any- en afirmativo o doble negaci\\u00f3n."	Any- en afirmativo = cualquiera: Anyone can do it.	1	2026-02-04 05:00:47.098987
318	21	Some, Any, No, Every Compounds	Grammar with compound pronouns	Gramática de pronombres compuestos:\n\nTodos son SINGULARES (aunque signifiquen "todos"):\n- Everyone IS here. (no "are")\n- Nobody KNOWS. (no "know")\n- Something HAS happened. (no "have")\n\nELSE después de compuestos:\n- someone else = otra persona\n- anywhere else = en otro lugar\n- nothing else = nada más\n- Would you like something else?\n\nCon preposiciones (al final):\n- I need someone to talk to.\n- There's nothing to worry about.\n- Do you have anywhere to stay?	Somebody has taken my pen. Is there anywhere else to go?	\N	"Everyone wants something different. Nothing lasts forever."	"Everyone are happy. \\u274c Something have changed. \\u274c"	"Usar verbo plural con pronombres compuestos."	En inglés informal: "Everyone brought their books" (their como género neutro)	2	2026-02-04 05:00:47.100416
319	22	Too and Enough	TOO + adjective = more than necessary (negative)	TOO antes del adjetivo (exceso negativo):\n\nTOO + adjective:\n- It's too hot. (demasiado calor)\n- He's too young to drive.\n- This is too expensive for me.\n\nTOO + adverb:\n- You speak too fast.\n- She arrived too late.\n\nTOO MUCH + uncountable noun:\n- There's too much sugar.\n- You have too much work.\n\nTOO MANY + countable noun:\n- There are too many people.\n- I have too many emails.\n\nTOO + adj/adv + TO + infinitive:\n- It's too cold to swim.\n- He was too tired to continue.	The music is too loud. I have too much homework.	\N	"It's too late to call. There are too many mistakes."	"It's too much cold. \\u274c Too people. \\u274c"	"Confundir too much/too many."	Too también puede ser "también": I'm tired too.	1	2026-02-04 05:00:47.101785
320	22	Too and Enough	ENOUGH + noun / adjective + ENOUGH = sufficient	ENOUGH para cantidad suficiente:\n\nENOUGH + noun (antes):\n- I have enough money.\n- There isn't enough time.\n- Do we have enough food?\n\nAdjective + ENOUGH (después):\n- She's old enough to vote.\n- Is it warm enough?\n- The coffee isn't hot enough.\n\nAdverb + ENOUGH:\n- She doesn't speak clearly enough.\n- Did you sleep well enough?\n\nAdj/Adv + ENOUGH + TO + infinitive:\n- He's tall enough to play basketball.\n- She's not fast enough to win.\n\nENOUGH + noun + TO:\n- I have enough time to finish.	Is this box big enough? I don't have enough experience.	\N	"She's smart enough to understand. There's enough space."	"She's enough old. \\u274c Enough big. \\u274c"	"Posici\\u00f3n incorrecta de enough."	Funnily enough = curiosamente (expresión fija)	2	2026-02-04 05:00:47.103432
321	23	Make vs Do	MAKE for creating/producing, DO for actions/tasks	MAKE = crear, producir, causar:\n\nExpresiones con MAKE:\n- make breakfast/lunch/dinner\n- make coffee/tea\n- make a decision/choice\n- make a mistake\n- make money\n- make friends\n- make a phone call\n- make plans\n- make progress\n- make an effort\n- make a promise\n- make noise\n- make someone happy/sad/angry\n\nMAKE + object + adjective:\n- The news made me happy.\n- You make me laugh.	I'm making dinner. She made a mistake. Don't make noise!	\N	"He makes good coffee. They made a decision."	"I'm doing dinner. \\u274c She did a mistake. \\u274c"	"Usar do con expresiones que llevan make."	Make the bed (aunque no creas la cama)	1	2026-02-04 05:00:47.105207
322	23	Make vs Do	DO for activities, duties, work	DO = realizar actividades, deberes:\n\nExpresiones con DO:\n- do homework/housework\n- do the dishes/laundry\n- do exercise/sport\n- do a job/work\n- do business\n- do your best\n- do a favor\n- do damage/harm\n- do well/badly\n- do an exam/test\n- do research\n\nDO con actividades indefinidas:\n- What are you doing?\n- I have nothing to do.\n- What do you do? (trabajo)\n\nDO como auxiliar:\n- Do you like it?\n- I don't know.	I need to do my homework. She does exercise every day.	\N	"Can you do me a favor? He did well on the test."	"I need to make my homework. \\u274c She makes exercise. \\u274c"	"Usar make con expresiones que llevan do."	Do your hair/makeup (arreglarse)	2	2026-02-04 05:00:47.106491
323	24	Phrasal Verbs Basics	Verb + particle changes meaning	Phrasal verbs comunes:\n\nLOOK:\n- look for = buscar (I'm looking for my keys)\n- look after = cuidar (She looks after her grandmother)\n- look forward to = esperar con ilusión\n\nGET:\n- get up = levantarse\n- get on/off = subir/bajar (transporte)\n- get along = llevarse bien\n\nTURN:\n- turn on/off = encender/apagar\n- turn up/down = subir/bajar (volumen)\n- turn around = darse vuelta\n\nTAKE:\n- take off = despegar/quitarse ropa\n- take out = sacar\n- take care of = cuidar	Please turn off the light. I'm looking for a job.	\N	"She gets up at 7 AM. The plane took off on time."	"Please off turn the light. \\u274c"	"Separar incorrectamente o usar la part\\u00edcula equivocada."	Algunos phrasal verbs son separables: turn it off / turn off the TV	1	2026-02-04 05:00:47.10755
324	24	Phrasal Verbs Basics	Separable vs inseparable phrasal verbs	Phrasal verbs separables e inseparables:\n\nSEPARABLES (el objeto puede ir en medio):\n- turn on the TV = turn the TV on = turn it on\n- pick up the book = pick the book up = pick it up\n- put on your coat = put your coat on = put it on\n\n⚠️ Con pronombres, DEBE separarse:\n- Turn it on. ✓\n- Turn on it. ✗\n\nINSEPARABLES (el objeto va después):\n- look after the baby ✓ (look the baby after ✗)\n- get on the bus ✓ (get the bus on ✗)\n- look for my keys ✓ (look my keys for ✗)\n\nInseparables comunes: look after, look for, get on, get off, come across, run into	I need to look after my sister. Please pick it up.	\N	"She takes after her mother. Turn it off, please."	"Look the children after. \\u274c Turn on it. \\u274c"	"Separar inseparables o no separar con pronombres."	Algunos pueden ser ambos según el significado.	2	2026-02-04 05:00:47.108548
325	37	Used to vs Would for Past Habits	USED TO for past states and habits	USED TO = solía (ya no es así):\n\nPara hábitos pasados:\n- I used to play tennis. (ya no juego)\n- She used to smoke. (ya no fuma)\n\nPara estados pasados:\n- I used to live in Paris. (ya no vivo)\n- He used to be thin. (ya no es)\n\nNegativo: didn't use to\n- I didn't use to like coffee.\n- She didn't use to exercise.\n\nPregunta: Did + subject + use to...?\n- Did you use to play sports?\n- Where did she use to work?\n\nImplica CAMBIO: la situación ya no existe.	I used to have long hair. We didn't use to have smartphones.	\N	"Did you use to live here? She used to be a teacher."	"I use to play. \\u274c I used to playing. \\u274c"	"Olvidar \\"d\\" en afirmativo o a\\u00f1adir \\"d\\" en negativo/pregunta."	BE USED TO + -ing = estar acostumbrado: I'm used to waking up early.	1	2026-02-04 05:00:47.110635
326	37	Used to vs Would for Past Habits	WOULD for repeated past actions only	WOULD para acciones habituales pasadas:\n\nSolo para acciones repetidas (NO estados):\n- Every summer, we would go to the beach.\n- She would always bring me coffee.\n- They would play chess every evening.\n\nNO usar WOULD con:\n- Estados: I would live in Paris. ❌ (use "used to")\n- Verbos de estado: She would know him. ❌\n- Situaciones no repetidas\n\nWOULD suena más nostálgico y literario:\n"When I was young, I would spend hours playing outside.\nMy grandmother would tell us stories by the fire."\n\nNecesita contexto temporal:\n- When I was a child, I would... ✓\n- I would go there. (sin contexto) ?	My father would read to us every night. We would go fishing on Sundays.	\N	"She would always laugh at his jokes."	"I would be a student. \\u274c (estado)"	"Usar would con verbos de estado o sin contexto."	Would también expresa voluntad pasada: He wouldn't listen to me.	2	2026-02-04 05:00:47.111783
327	38	Gerunds and Infinitives	Verbs followed by gerund (-ing)	Verbos + gerundio (-ing):\n\nENJOY + -ing:\n- I enjoy reading.\n- She enjoys traveling.\n\nAVOID + -ing:\n- Avoid eating too much sugar.\n- He avoids talking about it.\n\nOtros verbos + gerundio:\n- finish: I finished working at 6.\n- keep (on): Keep trying!\n- mind: Do you mind waiting?\n- suggest: I suggest leaving now.\n- consider: She's considering moving.\n- practice: Practice speaking English.\n- miss: I miss living there.\n- admit: He admitted stealing.\n- deny: She denied knowing him.\n- imagine: Imagine living on Mars!	I enjoy cooking. She avoids eating meat. Have you finished studying?	\N	"I don't mind helping. Consider applying for the job."	"I enjoy to cook. \\u274c She avoids to eat. \\u274c"	"Usar infinitivo con verbos que requieren gerundio."	Go + -ing para actividades: go swimming, go shopping	1	2026-02-04 05:00:47.112839
328	38	Gerunds and Infinitives	Verbs followed by infinitive (to + verb)	Verbos + infinitivo (to + verb):\n\nWANT + to:\n- I want to learn Spanish.\n- She wants to travel.\n\nDECIDE + to:\n- I decided to quit.\n- They decided to stay.\n\nOtros verbos + infinitivo:\n- hope: I hope to see you soon.\n- expect: I expect to finish today.\n- plan: We plan to visit London.\n- promise: She promised to help.\n- agree: He agreed to come.\n- refuse: They refused to pay.\n- learn: I'm learning to drive.\n- seem: She seems to be happy.\n- pretend: He pretended to sleep.\n- afford: I can't afford to buy it.\n- manage: She managed to escape.	I want to travel. She promised to call me. They agreed to help.	\N	"He hopes to get the job. I can't afford to go."	"I want traveling. \\u274c She promised calling. \\u274c"	"Usar gerundio con verbos que requieren infinitivo."	Help puede usar ambos: Help me (to) carry this.	2	2026-02-04 05:00:47.113906
329	38	Gerunds and Infinitives	Verbs that take both with different meanings	Verbos que cambian significado:\n\nSTOP:\n- stop doing = dejar de hacer\n  I stopped smoking. (ya no fumo)\n- stop to do = detenerse para hacer\n  I stopped to smoke. (me detuve para fumar)\n\nREMEMBER:\n- remember doing = recordar haber hecho\n  I remember meeting her. (recuerdo el encuentro)\n- remember to do = acordarse de hacer\n  Remember to call me. (no olvides)\n\nFORGET:\n- forget doing = olvidar haber hecho\n  I'll never forget seeing the Alps.\n- forget to do = olvidar hacer\n  I forgot to lock the door.\n\nTRY:\n- try doing = experimentar\n  Try eating less sugar.\n- try to do = intentar/esforzarse\n  I'm trying to learn Chinese.	I stopped eating meat. (quit) I stopped to eat. (paused to eat)	\N	"Remember to buy milk. I remember buying milk yesterday."	"Confundir los significados."	"No distinguir entre los dos significados."	El contexto ayuda a aclarar el significado.	3	2026-02-04 05:00:47.115123
330	39	Present Perfect Continuous	Form: have/has been + verb-ing	Formación del Present Perfect Continuous:\n\nEstructura: have/has + been + verb-ing\n\nAfirmativo:\n- I have been working.\n- She has been studying.\n- They have been waiting.\n\nNegativo: haven't/hasn't been + verb-ing\n- I haven't been sleeping well.\n- He hasn't been feeling well.\n\nPregunta: Have/Has + subject + been + verb-ing?\n- Have you been exercising?\n- How long has she been living here?\n\nContracción: I've been, she's been, they've been	I've been learning English for 3 years. She's been working all day.	\N	"Have you been waiting long? It's been raining since morning."	"I have been work. \\u274c She has being waiting. \\u274c"	"Olvidar been o -ing."	Verbos de estado usan Present Perfect Simple: I've known him for years.	1	2026-02-04 05:00:47.116912
331	39	Present Perfect Continuous	Use for duration and recent activities	Usos del Present Perfect Continuous:\n\n1. Acciones que empezaron en el pasado y continúan:\n   - I've been studying since 8 AM.\n   - She's been living here for 5 years.\n\n2. Acciones recientes con resultado visible:\n   - You're wet! Have you been swimming?\n   - My eyes hurt. I've been reading.\n\n3. Énfasis en la duración o continuidad:\n   - I've been waiting for an hour!\n   - How long have you been learning English?\n\nDiferencia con Present Perfect Simple:\n- Simple: enfatiza resultado/cantidad\n  I've written 3 emails. (resultado)\n- Continuous: enfatiza duración/proceso\n  I've been writing emails. (actividad)	Why are you tired? I've been working out. How long have you been waiting?	\N	"It's been snowing all day. She's been teaching for 20 years."	"I've been knowing him for years. \\u274c"	"Usar con verbos de estado."	Live/work pueden usar ambos: I've lived/been living here for 5 years.	2	2026-02-04 05:00:47.11821
332	52	Have/Get Something Done (Causative)	HAVE/GET + object + past participle for services	Causativo: alguien hace algo por ti:\n\nHAVE + object + past participle (más común):\n- I had my hair cut. (someone cut my hair)\n- She's having her car repaired.\n- We had our house painted.\n\nGET + object + past participle (más informal):\n- I got my phone fixed.\n- She's getting her nails done.\n- We got the windows cleaned.\n\nTiempos verbales:\n- Present: I have/get my car washed every week.\n- Past: I had my car washed yesterday.\n- Future: I'll have it done tomorrow.\n- Present Perfect: I've had it repaired.\n\nCon agente (menos común):\n- I had my hair cut by a new stylist.	I need to have my eyes tested. She got her dress cleaned.	\N	"We're having the kitchen remodeled. I got my photo taken."	"I had cut my hair. \\u274c I had someone cut my hair. (also correct, but different)"	"Confundir con el pasado perfecto."	GET + object + to infinitive = hacer que alguien haga algo: I got him to help me.	1	2026-02-04 05:00:47.120011
333	52	Have/Get Something Done (Causative)	MAKE vs LET vs HAVE + object + bare infinitive	Estructuras causativas con infinitivo sin TO:\n\nMAKE + object + bare infinitive (forzar/obligar):\n- She made me wait.\n- They made him apologize.\n- The movie made me cry.\n\nLET + object + bare infinitive (permitir):\n- Let me help you.\n- She let him go.\n- My parents let me stay out late.\n\nHAVE + object + bare infinitive (hacer que, organizar):\n- I'll have someone call you.\n- He had his assistant book the flight.\n\nEn pasivo, MAKE usa TO:\n- I was made to wait.\n- He was made to apologize.	Don't make me laugh. Let me explain. I'll have the secretary send it.	\N	"She made him clean his room. They let us leave early."	"She made him to clean. \\u274c Let me to help. \\u274c"	"A\\u00f1adir \\"to\\" despu\\u00e9s de make/let."	Allow usa to: She allowed him to leave.	2	2026-02-04 05:00:47.121067
334	53	Narrative Tenses	Combining Past Simple, Continuous, and Perfect	Tiempos narrativos para contar historias:\n\nPAST SIMPLE: acciones principales de la historia\n- I walked into the room. She saw me.\n\nPAST CONTINUOUS: contexto/escena de fondo\n- The sun was shining. People were walking.\n\nPAST PERFECT: acciones anteriores a la historia\n- I realized I had left my keys.\n- She was upset because he had lied.\n\nCombinación típica:\n"I was walking (background) down the street when I saw (main action) my ex-boyfriend. He was talking (background) to a woman I had never seen (earlier) before."\n\nMarcadores:\n- When = Past Simple después\n- While = Past Continuous después\n- After/Before = Past Perfect posible	I was sleeping when the phone rang. I had forgotten to set my alarm.	\N	"She realized she had made a mistake."	"I walked when the phone was ringing. \\u274c"	"No usar Past Perfect para la acci\\u00f3n anterior."	En narrativa informal, Past Simple puede reemplazar Past Perfect.	1	2026-02-04 05:00:47.122213
335	53	Narrative Tenses	Past Perfect Continuous for background activities	Past Perfect Continuous para duración anterior:\n\nEstructura: had been + verb-ing\n\nUso: acción en progreso ANTES de otra acción pasada\n- I had been waiting for an hour when she arrived.\n- She was tired because she had been working all night.\n- They had been living there for 10 years before they moved.\n\nÉnfasis en duración hasta un punto pasado:\n- How long had you been learning English before you moved?\n- He had been smoking for 20 years when he finally quit.\n\nSeñales visibles de actividad reciente (en pasado):\n- His eyes were red. He had been crying.\n- The ground was wet. It had been raining.	I had been studying for hours when you called. She had been running—that's why she was out of breath.	\N	"They had been dating for 2 years before they got engaged."	"I had been wait for an hour. \\u274c"	"Olvidar \\"been\\" o \\"-ing\\"."	Verbos de estado usan Past Perfect Simple: I had known him for years.	2	2026-02-04 05:00:47.123267
336	54	Articles - Advanced Usage	THE with unique, specific, or previously mentioned nouns	THE - usos avanzados:\n\nCosas únicas:\n- the sun, the moon, the Earth, the universe\n- the President (of this country), the government\n\nCon superlativos y ordinales:\n- the best, the worst, the first, the last\n\nPaíses/lugares específicos:\n- the USA, the UK, the Netherlands (plurales/unidos)\n- the Amazon, the Nile (ríos)\n- the Pacific, the Atlantic (océanos)\n- the Alps, the Andes (cordilleras)\n\nCon adjetivos sustantivados (grupos):\n- the rich, the poor, the elderly, the unemployed\n\nInstituciones en contexto:\n- I'm going to the bank/the doctor/the movies.	The elderly need special care. We visited the Alps.	\N	"The first time I saw the Eiffel Tower, I was amazed."	"I'm going to bank. \\u274c I visited Alps. \\u274c"	"Omitir THE donde es necesario."	Países singulares sin the: France, Japan, Mexico	1	2026-02-04 05:00:47.124309
337	54	Articles - Advanced Usage	Zero article for general statements and abstracts	Sin artículo (zero article):\n\nConceptos generales/abstractos:\n- Life is beautiful. (la vida en general)\n- Love conquers all.\n- Time flies.\n\nPlural general:\n- Dogs are loyal. (perros en general)\n- Children need love.\n- Teachers work hard.\n\nActividades/deportes:\n- play tennis/football/chess\n- do yoga/exercise\n\nComidas del día:\n- have breakfast/lunch/dinner\n- after breakfast, before lunch\n\nTransporte con BY:\n- by car, by bus, by plane\n\nLugares con propósito:\n- go to school (como estudiante)\n- go to bed (para dormir)\n- go to church (para rezar)	Happiness is important. I love music. We had dinner at 7.	\N	"Children need education. I go to work by bus."	"The life is beautiful. \\u274c I play the tennis. \\u274c"	"A\\u00f1adir art\\u00edculos innecesarios."	Si es específico, usa THE: The children in this school are smart.	2	2026-02-04 05:00:47.125309
